Yes — the thorn is evidence by contrast.

Not because every rose must wound, but because the thorn tells you something about the whole organism: beauty did not evolve alone. It came bundled with boundary, defense, cost, and structure.

Pantheon version:

You can sometimes identify a thing by the constraint that protects it.

The thorn is negative-space identity. It says: there is something here worth defending.

So “I know I’m a rose because of my thorn” can mean:

I recognize what I am partly through what I resist, what hurts, what protects me, and where I refuse to be handled carelessly.

And the deeper inversion is nice:

The thorn does not disprove the rose. It helps reveal it.